1 NAME Diego /Fernández de Cabrera y Bobadilla/ III Conde de Chinchón
2 GIVN Diego
2 SURN Fernández de Cabrera y Bobadilla
2 NSFX III Conde de Chinchón
2 _MARNM Fernández de Cabrera y Bobadilla
2 NICK III Conde de Chinchón
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1536
2 ADDR
3 CTRY España
1 DEAT
2 DATE 23 SEP 1608
1 TITL III Conde de Chinchón
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Diego Fernández de Cabrera y Bobadilla''' was the first-born son of''' Pedro Fernández de Cabrera, 2nd Count of Chinchón''' since 1522, and of '''Mencía de la Cerda y Mendoza''', (aunt of the famous Ana de Mendo
2 CONC za, wife of Ruy Gómez de Silva.) 
2 CONT '''
2 CONT His siblings were''' 
2 CONT # Andrés Cabrera, who devoted himself to an ecclesiastical career and occupied the archbishopric of Zaragoza; 
2 CONT # Hernando, mentally incapacitated, who was confined to the manorial domains of the House, supported by an income left to him by his mother; 
2 CONT # Pedro, who devoted himself to arms, losing himself in the defence of the Goleta in 1573; 
2 CONT # Teresa, who married the Count of Lemos; 
2 CONT # Mencía, married to Martín Cortés, 2nd Marquis of the Valley, and 
2 CONT # Ana, who was a lady-in-waiting to the Queen.
2 CONT
2 CONT There is little news about Diego's early life, although it is known that he was born in the town of Chinchón. His first steps in the King's service were taken during wartime. Some chroniclers point to his participation in the mili
2 CONC tary campaigns against France in the fifties and in the relief of Mazalquivir in 1563. He later settled at the Court to assist his father in his duties, to the point that, in 1571, it was rumoured that he was going to transfer to 
2 CONC him the General Treasury of the Crown of Aragon, which gave him access to the councils of Aragon and Italy, and which his father had enjoyed since 1558, thanks to his relationship with the Portuguese Gómez de Silva. During this pe
2 CONC riod he was introduced to the intricacies of the Government and to palace intrigues and manoeuvres, in which he became a consummate specialist.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the year of his father's death, 1576, the struggle for power in the Castilian Court was going through a delicate moment, with the confrontation between two clearly antagonistic factions. On one side, the group headed by the sec
2 CONC retary Mateo Vázquez, who had inherited from Diego de Espinosa the entire team of servants and a confessionalist ideology specific to the Spanish Monarchy; and on the other side was the faction led by Antonio Pérez, the "Papist fa
2 CONC ction", whose political and religious ideology was identified with a universal Catholicism closely linked to the interests of Rome. Diego joined the circle of the secretary Vázquez de Leça from the very beginning.
2 CONT
2 CONT The inheritance that the new count received from his father consisted not only of the title and states, but of a certain political space, whose main asset lay in the enjoyment of the favor that the Monarch had granted to his fathe
2 CONC r, and in the close relationship that had united him to the King's personal secretary, Mateo Vázquez de Leça, head, at that time, of the so-called "Castilianist party". At the same time that he put his life and property at the ser
2 CONC vice of the King, the Count of Chinchón turned to him to ensure the succession in the Treasury of Aragon, which had acquired a significant political component in recent times. The secretary did not disappoint him, despite the dela
2 CONC y in the issuance of the title - end of 1576 - due to the fact that Philip II considered it appropriate to undertake a reform of the office, earning through his efforts the eternal gratitude of the new count.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the first few months he personally carried out the duties of his office with great zeal, learning about the different sources of income available to the Monarch in the Aragonese and Italian kingdoms, administering them to p
2 CONC romptly satisfy the Monarch's slightest requirements, as well as the technical knowledge of the trade, especially on the minting of coins, which provided him with a basis and some notions that ultimately served him to exercise alm
2 CONC ost absolute control over the issues affecting the Crown of Aragon and Italy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Between 1578 and 1583, benefiting in particular from his participation in the Portuguese campaign, Diego Fernández put all his efforts into royal service and the destruction of his political enemies, especially Antonio Pérez (Phil
2 CONC ip II decreed his arrest on 28 July 1579); efforts that raised him to the highest level of confidence of the Sovereign as a main component of the councils of Aragon and Italy, and participating in the deliberation of various gover
2 CONC nment matters.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was also appointed steward of the Burgundian House of the King, as his father had been, thus maintaining the custom, more than any other type of regulation, of granting this main palatine office to the treasurers of the Crown o
2 CONC f Aragon. As steward of the Royal House, he played a central role in the formation of the House of Prince Philip and the Infantas Isabel Clara Eugenia and Catalina Micaela. With such a willingness to serve, which the Monarch must 
2 CONC have undoubtedly appreciated, it is not surprising that his code name in the encrypted dispatches was "request".
2 CONT
2 CONT From the first months of 1584, the beginnings of a new way of dealing with government affairs were visible at the Court. The King began to require more direct participation in the administration from those who deserved his utmost 
2 CONC confidence.
2 CONT
2 CONT Through the practice of daily dispatches throughout this year, a governing body of a “political” nature was established, capable of directing the technicians, who were from then on confined to the Councils and Audiences. This situ
2 CONC ation was articulated a year later, after the celebration of the Cortes of Monzón, apparently following the recommendations of the Bishop of the Canary Islands, Melchor de Yebra, although it did not begin to be institutionalized a
2 CONC s an organ until the end of 1588 or beginning of 1589, being embodied for some time in what was called the “Junta de Noche”; which meant the return of the nobility to the positions that corresponded to them by right in a corporate
2 CONC  society.
2 CONT
2 CONT But it was not the high nobility, nor even a majority of titled nobility that formed part of this board: at first, Juan de Zúñiga, Juan de Idiáquez, Cristóbal de Moura and the third Count of Chinchón; with the later addition of th
2 CONC e Marquis of Velada and the Count of Fuensalida in the background, were its members. Thanks to this privileged position, he appeared, from then on, as a great court patron outside of his old friendship with the secretary Mateo Váz
2 CONC quez, relegated by the new government structures.
2 CONT
2 CONT As was to be expected, this personal and political progress made him the target of attacks by his adversaries, who had a very powerful weapon at their disposal.
2 CONT
2 CONT Reference is made here to the dubious lineage of Andrés Cabrera, 1st Marquis of Moya - the original lineage of the Counts of Chinchón, since the first count, Fernando, was the second son of Andrés Cabrera.
2 CONT
2 CONT Specifically, there were serious doubts about the purity of blood of Pedro López de Madrid, the Marquis's father.
2 CONT
2 CONT This shady business was used by his political rivals to cause him as much damage as possible, which had repercussions on his career, at a time when he was beginning to stand out at Court, influencing, for example, negatively his e
2 CONC ntry into the Council of State. In February 1589, the purity of his family's blood was officially declared, although the count's uneasiness was palpable when he tried, days after the death of Mateo Vázquez, to recover the papers o
2 CONC f this business in order, as Philip II himself made clear, to "burn them [because] I think that is why he must want them."
2 CONT
2 CONT During the life of the Prudent King, Diego de Cabrera y Bobadilla enjoyed royal favour and not only intervened in Aragonese and Italian affairs, despite the continued deterioration of the situation in the kingdom, but also partici
2 CONC pated in all kinds of matters, as shown by his entry into the Board of Works and Forests, where he came to control its activity, making his secretary Juan de Ibarra his debtor; in matters relating to the German guard and the corps
2 CONC  archers, in the preparation of the pragmatic in 1586 of the “Titles and Courtesies” in order to establish a regulation of the treatments that should be given according to rank and position, which was promoted by the members of th
2 CONC e “Castilianist” group to be used as a tool to increase their influence at Court and which merited the establishment of a special board from May 1583; in the drafting of the new ordinances of the Chamber of January 1588 and in var
2 CONC ious Morisco meetings, to name the most notable. Only the adventures of Antonio Pérez in Zaragoza around 1590, after fleeing from the castle of Turégano, and the death of his first-born son caused him deep displeasure, he fell ser
2 CONC iously ill and temporarily retired from government business. After recovering the prominence lost during the previous months, Chinchón took the reins of the so-called "Aragonese alterations" and, after the flight of Antonio Pérez 
2 CONC to France, the military intervention was resolved which meant the reestablishment of royal authority.
2 CONT
2 CONT Over the years, Fernández de Cabrero enjoyed many favours. He was appointed Treasurer of the Mint of Segovia and, after the death of the Duke of Alba, the Chief Steward of the Royal Household, in Lisbon in 1582, the King decided n
2 CONC ot to fill this post, so the Count, as one of the oldest stewards, carried out the duties of this important palatine office on a significant number of occasions. In any case, it seems that at least during the last decade of the re
2 CONC ign the only stewards active in the Court were the Counts of Fuensalida and Chinchón himself. In 1593 he was made a member of the Council of State, although he was denied a seat in the Council of War, he was integrated into the Go
2 CONC verning Board and was named the King's executor. A year later, after the death of Cardinal Quiroga, he held the presidency of the Council of Italy until 1596 on an interim basis. In that same year, on July 29, he was granted the M
2 CONC onreal commandery of the Order of Santiago, which had been left vacant by Juan de Idiáquez.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, the death of Philip II meant his fall from the leadership that governed the Spanish Monarchy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Chinchón became easy prey for the new patrons of the Court, who not only cornered him, but also used his involvement in the events in Aragon to subject him to a visit in which he was charged with a total of sixteen charges, despit
2 CONC e the fact that at first he was allowed to appear before the Council of War, within the profound renewal that the government underwent, and he maintained his position in the Royal House until he received the order on December 23, 
2 CONC 1601 to proceed with the audit of the activities of the treasurer of the House, Francisco Guillamás Velázquez. Although the task must not have been undertaken with too much enthusiasm and, on May 1, 1606, Philip III decided to cre
2 CONC ate a commission to take the accounts, formed by Pedro de Bañuelos, then accountant of income, Antonio González de Legarda, accountant of the ratio, and Mateo Ferro, lieutenant of the general treasurer of the Crown of Aragon.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, the lack of sympathy he generated in the Duke of Lerma, the restrictions on access to the Monarch and the universal hatred he had in Aragon led to his withdrawal, plunged into a deep depression due to the hostile environm
2 CONC ent that surrounded him, to his states for a certain time, being the member of the former team of rulers who came out worst off.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, after having remained close to the King for so many years, enjoying his "grace" and, consequently, exercising power with all the privileges and burdens that this entailed, and the desire to ensure the political future of 
2 CONC his heir, Luis Gerónimo, he could not resign himself to his new status, so he prepared to contribute again to the tasks of government, bowing to the new owners of the situation.
2 CONT
2 CONT He even went so far as to entrust in his will to the good will of the Dukes of Lerma and Cea the succession of all the offices that he had held. In this sense his efforts were not in vain, since in 1613, when he came of age, Luis 
2 CONC Gerónimo took possession of the post of general treasurer of the Crown of Aragon, beginning a long career that took him to the viceroyalty of Peru.
2 CONT
2 CONT In February 1602 he was acquitted of all the accusations levelled during the visit made to him in the government of Aragon's affairs. There were only three, testimonial, but they did him much harm: favouring one of his accountants
2 CONC  to be named canon of the Basilica of Pilar in Zaragoza, consulting with the King without the Council's agreement on the bishopric of Teruel and having delayed the provision of the post of general bailiff of Valencia. Nevertheless
2 CONC , he continued to attend the sessions of the organisations to which he belonged. He again entered regularly into the Councils of State and War, maintained his functions as treasurer of Aragon, although much more restrictive, as we
2 CONC ll as in the Board of Works and Forests, due to the extensive experience he had gathered, evidently without the slightest hint of the influence he had enjoyed during the previous century.
2 CONT
2 CONT But, despite his efforts to continue his work in the government of the Monarchy with the same vitality as always, Bobadilla was already an old man. Aware that his end was approaching, he made a will in Madrid on 12 May 1607 and ar
2 CONC ranged the conditions and place of his burial, in the main chapel of the church of Chinchón. He died in Madrid on 23 September 1608.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.academia.edu/62562366/Diego_Fern%C3%A1ndez_Cabrera_y_Bobadilla_and_the_Capilla_Mayor_at_Chinch%C3%B3n
