1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_Manuel_Fern%C3%A1ndez_Pacheco
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_Manuel_Mar%C3%ADa_de_la_Aurora,_8th_duke_of_Escalona
2 CONT
2 CONT Juan Manuel María de la Aurora Fernández Pacheco Acuña Girón y Portocarrero, grandee of Spain, 8th Duke of Escalona, 8th Marquis of Villena, 12th Count of San Esteban de Gormaz and 8th Count of Xiquena (Marcilla, Navarra, 7 Septem
2 CONC ber 1650 - Madrid, June 29, 1725), was a Spanish aristocrat, politician and academician.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of Diego López Pacheco, 7th Duke of Escalona (1599–1653).
2 CONT  He served as viceroy and captain-general of the Kingdoms of Navarre, Aragon, Catalonia, Sicily and Naples. He was awarded the title of Knight of Order of the Golden Fleece on 9 October 1687.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the War of Spanish Succession, he was imprisoned in Gaeta, Naples, by the Austrian empire after losing the Siege of Gaeta (1707). Upon his release and return to Spain, he founded (under orders of King Phillip V) the Royal S
2 CONC panish Academy or Real Academia Española (RAE). He was elected its 1st lifetime Director in 1713. He was also Mayordomo mayor to the King.
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes on ancestry
2 CONT
2 CONT This Pacheco surname comes from Portugal and it has had a quite complicated, calculating, troublesome and violent political history in the Spanish Kingdom since the beginnings of the XV Century, with a great deal of "profitable ma
2 CONC rriages" meanwhile.
2 CONT
2 CONT Juan Pacheco, (Belmonte 1419 - Trujillo 1 November 1474), was "Rico-hombre de Castilla", 3rd señor de Belmonte, 1st marquiss de Villena (12 July 1445), by King Juan II of Castile, 1st . Duke of ESCALONA (17 December 1472) by King 
2 CONC Enrique IV of Castile, and many other minor titles, professed as a Knight on the Military Order of Santiago having the approval from the Roman Pope on 15 November 1453 as he had been previously married.
2 CONT
2 CONT He managed, through great fights between opposite groups, to be "elected" the 39th "Maestre of the Order of Santiago " with the approval in 1467, but apparently no Papal approval, of the short lived and much younger step brother o
2 CONC f King Enrique IV, called Prince Alfonso, chosen as "Puppet King" of Castile by a sector of the Nobility.
2 CONT
2 CONT This unfortunate prince, the utherine brother of the later Queen Isabel I of Castile, lasted a few months being, perhaps poisoned, aged around 16 .
2 CONT
2 CONT With ambition and treacheries this Juan Pacheco kept a lot of fights and troubles with other nobility fractions dying in 1474.
2 CONT
2 CONT His first descendents were closely controlled by the Catholic Monarchs but by 1520, King of Imperial Spain Carlos I of Spain had promoted some of them as Grandees of Spain, Carlos being the grandson of the above mentioned Reyes Ca
2 CONC tolicos.
2 CONT
2 CONT With the great amounts of land and income from "the Pacheco´s block" commercial ventures, Carlos I of Spain, seeking the money to be able to purchase fidelities of the Germanic Great Electors, against would be Emperor of the Germa
2 CONC nic Holy Roman Empire, Francis I of France, and with the flow of gold and silver flooding the whole of Europe from America, it is easy to understand why we speak today also of Charles V Habsburg, Emperor of the Germanic Holy Roman
2 CONC  Empire, too. In spite of this, Money, Finances and History writers choose however to keep apart, it seems.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shifting loyalties and troublesome Pacheco's managed also to get Royal Approval to have his male children "create new lineages uing former glorious historical names from extinct lines, because of female successions". Therefore "ne
2 CONC w names" came back into the High Spanish Nobility seeking adequate females to get married along different times, since as early as 1385, such as Portocarrero, Girón, Téllez-Girón and/or, changing and coming back from grand parents
2 CONC  to grandsons, Fernández Pacheco/ then López Pacheco/then Fernandez Pacheco/then Lopez Pacheco again and so on.
2 CONT
2 CONT Females, and illegitimates used Pacheco only many times but it was not rare for families such as the ubiquitous and powerful Mendoza, to hide a Mendoza woman, wife of a conflictive Pacheco describing her as a "Pacheco" nowadays pr
2 CONC obably to hide the embarrassment of a Mendoza woman associated with any socially conflictive or notoriously rebellious Pacheco.
2 CONT
2 CONT Trying an approach to later Pacheco's
2 CONT
2 CONT We will refer here only to the 6th, and 7th Dukes of Escalona, ancestors of the 8th, 9th and 10th Dukes of Escalona, who were respectively 1st, 2nd and 3rd Directors of the Royal Spanish Academy, Juan Manuel, Mercurio Antonio and 
2 CONC Andrés María respectively Directors between 1713 and 1746
2 CONT
2 CONT Meetings of the 24 strong Selected Group of "Academicians", quite a hard working lot indeed, even for today technical standards, took place at the Madrid Private Palaces of these 3 Escalona's Dukes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Political and military background of the first 3 family related Royal Spanish Academy Directors
2 CONT
2 CONT Don Juan Manuel father, the 7th Duke of Escalona, between other titles, had the name Diego Roque López Pacheco, (Alcázar de Belmonte, (16 August 1599, the day of Saint Roque in the Calendar - + Pamplona 27 February 1653, aged 52),
2 CONC  a Knight of the Order of the Golden Fleece, and had married first in 1620 his cousin Luisa Bernarda de Cabrera y Bobadilla, but she died in 1638, 7th Marchioness of Moya, a title awarded by the Catholic Monarchs, Isabel I of Cast
2 CONC ile and Ferdinand II of Aragon to a family of Crown treasurers and financial royal accountants converted from Judaism to Catholicism .
2 CONT
2 CONT The 7th Duke, Diego Roque, was sent then, as a widower, as a Viceroy and Captain General of Mexico, (1638 - detained and put in prison by Royal Appointee as New Viceroy Bishop Juan de Palafox y Mendoza in 1642), marrying then agai
2 CONC n, in 1644, the lady described as Juana María Francisca de Zuñiga, (deceased at Pamplona, Navarre, on 17 February 1652), daughter of powerful Francisco Diego Lopez de Zuñiga y Sotomayor, 7th Duke of Bejar.
2 CONT
2 CONT The reason by widower Diego Roque, 7th Duke of Escalona was detained in Mexico when he was the 17th Viceroy was the high suspicions in the Madrid Court concerning the Portuguese Family of the powerful Dukes of Braganza.
2 CONT
2 CONT The 7th Duque Teodosio II de Braganza (1568-Duke 1583-1630), had taken part, aged around 11, in the significant North African battle known as Battle of Alcazarquivir.
2 CONT
2 CONT Portugal had become, as a consequence, a Kingdom ruled by King Philip II of Spain, being called there Philip I of Portugal. He took a lot of trouble however in getting both nobilities, Spanish and Portuguese, hating one another, a
2 CONC s quite, peaceful and no mixings or misunderstandings as best as he could.
2 CONT
2 CONT The amounts of lands in Asia, Africa, America and Europe was thus staggering when both powers were ruled by a same physical King and successful management must have been most difficult indeed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Then, Duke Teodosio's II son, a cousin of the 7th Duke of Escalona and Viceroy of Mexico till his imprisonment in 1642 in Mexico, 8th Duke Juan II de Braganza (1604-Duke successor 1630-New King of Portugal after 1 December 1640- 1
2 CONC 656), was crowned and accepted as King of Portugal, as the first King of the new Braganza Royal Dynasty.
2 CONT
2 CONT New King Juan II of Portugal since 1 December 1640 was married to the daughter of the Duke of Medinasidonia, accused of having tried to set up and Independent Andalusia. The new Spanish born Portuguese Queen got her father arreste
2 CONC d and the Duke of Medinasidonia died while in prison.
2 CONT
2 CONT This, one of many obscure pages of the then powerful but exhausted by widely extended military investments and wars Habsburg Imperial Spain has received, and will continue receiving, no doubt, the attention of World Historians.
2 CONT
2 CONT Therefore, the 7th Duke of Escalona, the suspected Mexico Viceroy replaced by the Madrid Court forecasters, who had inherited from the 6th Duke, (deceased without issue), died, 1653, when his son Juan Manuel was barely three years
2 CONC  old, and while his 2nd wife, the daughter of the 7th Duke of Bejar, the mother of Juan Manuel, had died the year before, 1652.
2 CONT
2 CONT Fortunately, the 6th Duke of Escalona, albeit dying without issue, have had several bastard brothers/sisters who had professed in the Holy Religion, namely the ones described as:
2 CONT
2 CONT 6Esc. B1) Diego Antonio Pacheco, Dean of Jaén Cathedral, (20 September 1594 - a prisonier in Istambul, (Turkey), in 1616, aged 22 - ?).
2 CONT
2 CONT 6Esc. B2) Juan Francisco Pacheco, (baptized at Rome, Italy, 28 February 1606)), a Knight of the Military Order of San Juan, Fellow student at the Colegio Mayor de los Caballeros Manriques, at Alcalá de Henares, Canon and Dean at J
2 CONC aén on 17 April 1622, sommelier of King Philip IV, Bishop of Cuenca, 1654, Bishop of Córdoba, 1658 - deceased 1663). This Bishop had no less than two children, a nun, Juana Maria, resident at a Convent in Escalona, and Andres Maur
2 CONC icio, Cavalry Captain in the Spanish Governor ship of Milano Dukedom, Italy, deceased in Rome, Italy, 1654.
2 CONT
2 CONT 6Esc. B3) Maria Pacheco, Abbess of the Concepcion Convent at Escalona, (Toledo), where her bastard niece, daughter of his bastard brother, the later Bishop of Cuenca and Córdoba afore mentioned, was a nun.
2 CONT
2 CONT Furthermore, the 7th Duke of Escalona, itself, got another bastard son in Holy Religion, named
2 CONT
2 CONT 7Esc. B1) Antonio Pacheco, "colegial mayor en el Mayor de los Caballeros Manriques de Alcalá de Henares, deán y canónigo de la Santa Iglesia de Cuenca - + Cuenca 2.3.1676)".
2 CONT
2 CONT As a bastard brother of the 8th Duke, Juan Manuel, he did not only have then bastard relative ancestors but also this bastard brother, Antonio.
2 CONT
2 CONT He got from his bastard brother, also two nephews, the fruit of the sins of this Canon and Deanat Cuenca, Antonio:
2 CONT
2 CONT 7Esc. B1. 1) Juan Antonio Pacheco, engaged in military actions in Flanders.
2 CONT
2 CONT 7Esc. B1. 2) Juana María Pacheco, Abbess of the Concepcion Monastery at Escalona, as another bastard female ancestor, + 1674.
2 CONT
2 CONT Clearly enough, his bastard relatives, spanning several generations, did everything they could to educate the fragile and nearly a baby Don Juan Manuel, the orphaned baby 8th Duke. They excelled in it, apparently .
2 CONT
2 CONT The descendency:the 2nd Director of the Royal Spanish Academy
2 CONT
2 CONT There is description, above, of Don Juan Manuel, 8th Duke, military and cultural assignments:
2 CONT
2 CONT His son, named slightly different, Mercurio López Pacheco y Benavides, (Escalona 9 May 1679 - + Madrid 7 June 1738), was the 9th Duke of Escalona and many other titles and honours being the lifetime 2nd director of the RAE (1725–1
2 CONC 738).
2 CONT
2 CONT The 1st and 2nd Directors' descendents
2 CONT
2 CONT His grandson, who was named Andrés María Fernández Pacheco y Moscoso, (Madrid, 13 August 1710 - Member of the Royal Spanish Academy 25 April 1726, aged 16 - married 21 October 1727, aged 17 - Third Director 1738, aged 28 - 27 June
2 CONC  1746, aged 36), was the 10th Duke of Escalona, twice a Grandee of Spain and many other titles.
2 CONT
2 CONT Note the shifts on the Pacheco names branch: López/Fernández/López between the three generations as granted by the feeble Kings spanning the end of the XIV and the beginning of the 15th century, (1379–1474), Castilian throne: Juan
2 CONC  I, Enrique III, Juan II, Enrique IV.
