1 OCCU Capitan, Fundador de Laredo, Spanish Captain who founded Loredo, Texas, Founder of Laredo
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Founder of Laredo, Texas 15 of May, 1755
2 CONT
2 CONT From the English Wikipedia page:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Don_Tom%C3%A1s_S%C3%A1nchez
2 CONT
2 CONT Don Tomás Sánchez de Barrera y de la Garza was a veteran Spanish Captain who founded Laredo, Texas, United States and Nuevo Laredo, Tamaulipas, Mexico, at the time it was one town in the Nuevo Santander Province.
2 CONT
2 CONT Origins
2 CONT
2 CONT Capitan Tomás Sánchez de la Barrera, was born into northern Mexico's oligarchic families. His family roots are traced into the old "hidalgo" families of Andalucia, Spain, and from his mother's side, he came from the powerful de la
2 CONC  Garza family, which controlled a great majority of the land from Corpus Christi to Saltillo, and held important government offices in most of the north of the Viceroyalty of the New Spain.
2 CONT
2 CONT Founding of Laredo
2 CONT
2 CONT He first arrived in 1749 to a place he named "El Paso de Jacinto", later called "Indian Ford", which is now west of Downtown Laredo. He then petitioned Colonel José de Escandón, 1st Count of Sierra Gorda, for permission to found a
2 CONC  town at this location. His petition was granted on May 15, 1755. He named the new townsite, Villa de San Agustin de Laredo, in honor to Saint Augustine and Colonel Escandon's hometown Laredo, Cantabria, Spain. Don Tomás Sánchez f
2 CONC irst established three families in the new villa. The site selected is located in the San Agustin Historical District in Downtown Laredo near the San Agustin Cathedral.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Footnote:
2 CONT *1. City of Laredo; A Brief History of Laredo, Texas
2 CONT ** http://www.ci.laredo.tx.us/Sanchez/TSanchez.html
2 CONT
2 CONT ====================================
2 CONT
2 CONT From Additional Source:
2 CONT
2 CONT Tomás Sánchez de la Barrera y Garza, founder of Laredo, son of Tomás Sánchez and María Josefa de la Garza, was born in the Valle de Carrizal near Monterrey, Nuevo León, in 1709. As a young man he served in the army of his king and
2 CONC  later ran a ranch in Coahuila. When civilization pushed northward to the Rio Grande and José Vásquez Borregoqv established his ranch and headquarters at the Hacienda de Nuestra Señora de los Dolores on the north bank, Sánchez ope
2 CONC ned a ranch on the south side within sight of the new settlement. He was residing there in 1754, when he petitioned José de Escandón for permission to found a town on the north bank of the river. Escandón wanted a settlement on th
2 CONC e Nueces and told Sánchez to explore that area to determine the feasibility of establishing a colony there. After a reconnaissance Sánchez reported that he found no site fit for settlement and repeated his request for permission t
2 CONC o locate a villa ten leagues north of Dolores near Paso de Jacinto, later called the "Old Indian Crossing," near Fort McIntosh. Escandón approved the request and appointed Sánchez captain and chief justice of the new settlement, t
2 CONC o be named Laredo. On May 15, 1755, Sánchez founded Laredo with his family and several others. In 1767, after settlers had received ranchlands and a charter from the Spanish crown, they elected as alcalde José Martínez de Sotomaye
2 CONC r; but because of his lack of courage to defend against Indian attacks and his attempt to remove the settlers to the south of the Rio Grande, he was removed from office and replaced by Sánchez. Sánchez was almost singly responsibl
2 CONC e for maintaining the settlement on the north bank of the Rio Grande, and he held the offices of chief justice and alcalde with only brief intermissions until his death in January 1796. He married Catalina (Catherina) Uribe, and t
2 CONC hey had nine children. With his second wife, Teodora Yzaguirre, he had two children. On October 16, 1938, the Texas Centennial Commission erected a monument marking the site of the founding of Laredo by Tomás Sánchez
2 CONT
2 CONT Citation:
2 CONT Winifred W. Vigness, "SANCHEZ DE LA BARRERA Y GARZA, TOMAS," Handbook of Texas Online (http://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/online/articles/fsa19), accessed April 22, 2014. Uploaded on June 15, 2010. Published by the Texas State His
2 CONC torical Association
2 CONT
2 CONT _________________________________________________________________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT CAPTAIN TOMAS-TADEO5 SANCHEZ-DE-LA-BARRERA (TOMAS4, TOMAS3 SANCHEZ-DURAN, FRANCISCO2 SANCHEZ-DE-LA-BARRERA, JUAN1 SANCHEZ-Y-ORTEGA) 
2 CONT *was born June 04, 1709 in Cienega de Flores, Nuevo Leon, Mexico, and 
2 CONT *died January 21, 1796 in Laredo, Webb County, Texas. 
2 CONT *He married (1) CATALINA DE URIBE-TREVINO April 11, 1729 in Monterrey, Nuevo Leon, Mexico, 
2 CONT **daughter of PHELIPE DE URIBE-DE-LA-CADENA-LOBO and MARIA DE TREMINO-DIAZ-NAVARRO. She was born Bet. 1708 - 1709. 
2 CONT *He married (2) TEODORA YZAGUIRRE Abt. 1760. 
2 CONT **She died October 21, 1825 in Laredo, Webb County, Texas.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of TOMAS-TADEO SANCHEZ-DE-LA-BARRERA and CATALINA DE URIBE-TREVINO are:
2 CONT *1. i. TOMAS6 SANCHEZ-DE-URIBE, 
2 CONT **b. July 11, 1730, Nuestra Sra de Guadalupe, Salinas Victoria, Nuevo Leon, Mexico.
2 CONT *2. 43. ii. MARIA-JOSEFA SANCHEZ-DE-URIBE, 
2 CONT **b. March 13, 1731/32, Nuestre Sr. de Guadalupe, Salinas Victoria, Nuevo Leon, Mexico.
2 CONT *3. iii. BLAS-DE-JESUS SANCHEZ-URIBE, 
2 CONT **b. March 07, 1735/36, Nuestra Sra de Guadalupe, Salinas Victoria, Nuevo Leon, Mexico; 
2 CONT **m. FRANCISCA FLORES, Abt. 1760, Nuevo Leon, Mexico.
2 CONT *4. 44. iv. MARIA-GERTRUDIS SANCHEZ-DE-URIBE, 
2 CONT **b. October 26, 1737, Nuestra Sra de Guadalupe, Salinas Victoria, Nuevo Leon, Mexico.
2 CONT *5. 45. v. TOMASA SANCHEZ-DE-URIBE, 
2 CONT **b. July 11, 1740, Cienega de Flores, Nuevo Leon, Mexico.
2 CONT *6. 46. vi. JOSEPH-EUGENIO-DE-LA-CANDELARIA SANCHEZ-DE-URIBE, 
2 CONT **b. December 30, 1741, Nuestra Sra de Guadalupe, Salinas Victoria, Nuevo Leon, Mexico.
2 CONT *7. 47. vii. MARIA-ALEXANDRA SANCHEZ-DE-LA-BARRERA, 
2 CONT **b. March 13, 1741/42, San Juan Bautista, Lampazos de Naranjo, Nuevo Leon, Mexico; 
2 CONT **d. September 14, 1814, Mier, Tamaulipas, Mexico.
2 CONT *8. 48. viii. ANTONIO-DIONICIO SANCHEZ-DE-URIBE, 
2 CONT **b. November 08, 1748, Lampazos, Nuevo Leon, Mexico.
2 CONT *9. 49. ix. JOSEPH-ENRIQUEZ SANCHEZ-DE-URIBE, 
2 CONT **b. Abt. 1750, Nuevo Leon, Mexico.
2 CONT *10. 50. x. TOMAS-ANTONIO SANCHEZ-DE-URIBE, 
2 CONT **b. January 11, 1749/50, San Juan Bautista, Lampazos, Nuevo Leon, Mexico.
2 CONT *11. 51. xi. SANTIAGO-DE-JESUS SANCHEZ-DE-URIBE, 
2 CONT **b. Abt. 1754, Monterrey, Nuevo Leon, Mexico; 
2 CONT **d. November 10, 1807, Laredo, Webb County, Texas.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of TOMAS-TADEO SANCHEZ-DE-LA-BARRERA and TEODORA YZAGUIRRE are:
2 CONT *1. xii. MARIA-ANDREA6 SANCHEZ-YZAGUIRRE, 
2 CONT **b. Laredo, Webb County, Texas; 
2 CONT **m. JOSE MARIA TOVAR-FLORES, February 28, 1810, San Agustin, Laredo, Webb County, Texas; 
2 CONT ***b. Guajuquilla.
2 CONT *2. xiii. TOMAS SANCHEZ-YZAGUIRRE, 
2 CONT **m. FRANCISCA GARCIA.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source:
2 CONT *In the book, Mil Familias III, by Rodolfo Gonzalez de la Garza, he is listed as a descendent of the Don Alonso of Estrada, Duke of Aragon and the Royal Treasurer and Governor of New Spain, (Mexico) and the Onate Family of Zacatec
2 CONC as and New Mexico. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT On May 15, 1755, he founded the town of San Agustin de Laredo (Laredo Texas).
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1767 he received a royal charter (Porcion 10 & 11) from the king of Spain. 10,628 acres located in Webb County.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: 
2 CONT *Ranches and Ranching in Spanish Texas by Lyle W. Williams. Page 8
2 CONT *Handbook on Texas Online.
2 CONT *He and his family are listed on the July 22, 1757 census for Laredo, Texas.
2 CONT *Webb County Family Histories Volume I, Page 233.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage Notes for TOMAS-TADEO SANCHEZ-DE-LA-BARRERA and CATALINA DE URIBE-TREVINO:
2 CONT *Index Marriage Investigation from the Diocese of Guadalajara, by Raul J. Guerra, Jr., Nadine M. Vasquez, Baldomero Vela, Jr. Page 258.
2 CONT
2 CONT =======
